WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:05.280
What shocking death is set to reshape the&nbsp;
future of the entire Marvel Cinematic Universe?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:08.480
Who turned out to be someone other&nbsp;
than who they said they were?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:11.200
Multiple times? Keep watching to find out!

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:17.040
The big reveal in "Iron Man 3" that the Mandarin's&nbsp;
true identity was not who it appeared to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:22.800
became a polarizing moment in MCU history. Love&nbsp;
it or hate it, the moment was one most viewers did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:29.040
not see coming – and nearly a decade later, would&nbsp;
become a major plot point in a different MCU film.

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:32.800
When trailers were initially released for&nbsp;
the third film in the "Iron Man" series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:37.200
fans learned the Mandarin would be joining&nbsp;
the franchise; a longtime comic book adversary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:41.200
for Iron Man, his appearance felt as&nbsp;
inevitable as it was straightforward.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:46.160
Yet, Mandarin had a slippery history when it&nbsp;
came to his racial origins in old comic books,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:50.320
and the casting of Oscar winner Sir Ben&nbsp;
Kingsley seemed to indicate, for some,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:54.640
a possible whitewashing of the character&nbsp;
that might just make matters worse.

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:58.880
Ultimately, Robert Downey Jr.'s Tony Stark&nbsp;
discovered the supposed leader of the Ten&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:04.400
Rings was an actor named Trevor Slattery, hired&nbsp;
to terrorize Stark in the guise of the Mandarin.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:09.040
A self-centered coward who saw the Mandarin&nbsp;
as "just a role," he was a smokesecreen for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:14.320
Aldrich Killian, the ultimate mastermind behind&nbsp;
this particular Mandarin terrorist threat.

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:18.160
While fans were torn about this&nbsp;
re-imagining of Iron Man's arch-nemesis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:23.360
many enjoyed Slattery's antics, and a Marvel&nbsp;
One-Shot short depicting his life in prison&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:28.080
and subsequent abduction by the "real"&nbsp;
Mandarin was well-received. Consequently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:32.720
Marvel surprised fans once again a decade&nbsp;
later — by making Kingsley's Slattery a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:38.080
significant supporting character in 2021's&nbsp;
"Shang-Chi and the Legend of the Ten Rings,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:42.560
continuing his storyline as a prisoner of the&nbsp;
title character's father, the real Mandarin.

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:48.800
As the MCU came to dominate the box office&nbsp;
throughout the 2010s, Marvel also made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:54.320
inroads via a mostly separate but parallel&nbsp;
world of TV, releasing series like "Agents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:59.360
of SHIELD" on broadcast networks and streaming&nbsp;
platforms. While "SHIELD" sometimes reacted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:04.400
events in Marvel movies or featured cameos by&nbsp;
the likes of Samuel L. Jackson as Nick Fury, a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:10.160
partnership with Netflix yielded six interrelated&nbsp;
series that had even less to do with the MCU

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:12.480
Despite not being closely tied to the movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:17.280
though, shows like "Daredevil" were well&nbsp;
received. But at the beginning of 2019,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:21.920
the Netflix production partnership was&nbsp;
dissolved and all the series were canceled.

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:26.480
One of the casualties was Vincent D'Onofrio's&nbsp;
portrayal of the classic Marvel supervillain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:31.600
Kingpin, which had earned rave reviews from&nbsp;
fans and critics alike. But it turned out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:37.280
that the folks at Marvel Studios were also big&nbsp;
fans of D'Onofrio, because in December 2021,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:43.120
he made a surprise debut in the MCU proper as&nbsp;
a major figure in the Disney+ show "Hawkeye."

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:47.680
That was followed almost immediately by&nbsp;
"Daredevil" star Charlie Cox making a cameo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:52.320
as Matt Murdock in "No Way Home," opening&nbsp;
the door for other discarded Netflix heroes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:56.560
to make their return, and cementing&nbsp;
those shows as official MCU canon.

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:01.280
"Avengers: Age of Ultron" may not be&nbsp;
the most warmly-regarded MCU outing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:05.040
but it did offer some unexpected&nbsp;
plot points that would shape the MCU.

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:10.160
Chief among them was the death of Pietro&nbsp;
Maximoff, brother of Wanda — aka Quicksilver&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:14.320
and Scarlet Witch — who spent most of&nbsp;
the movie battling against the Avengers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:17.760
but ultimately became sympathetic&nbsp;
characters. Fans were as shocked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:21.200
as Hawkeye when Pietro gave his&nbsp;
life to save the avenging archer.

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:22.800
"Bet you didn't see that coming."

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:31.760
Much like the Mandarin, this plotline&nbsp;
would be revisited nearly a decade later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:35.840
much to the surprise of MCU fans,&nbsp;
as Wanda's trauma over Pietro's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:39.760
death became a major plot point&nbsp;
in the Disney+ show "WandaVision."

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:45.360
Oh, and it also led to the dumbest fakeout in&nbsp;
MCU history, as Evan Peters' alternate universe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:50.880
version of Pietro turned out to be just some&nbsp;
random guy. C'mon, MCU, you can do better!

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:59.840
"You're… Ralph Bohner?" "Boner."

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:05.040
Peter Quill is essentially an overgrown adolescent&nbsp;
frozen in the intergalactic fantasies of an '80s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:10.240
kid who watched too many movies. His obsession&nbsp;
with Earth pop culture — fueled largely by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:14.800
mixtapes his mother left him before her death&nbsp;
— has him dancing his way through heists and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:18.800
other adventures of the "Guardians&nbsp;
of the Galaxy" and wider MCU films.

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:24.000
However, when he became caught up in a galactic&nbsp;
battle between Xandar's Nova Corps and Ronan the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:29.200
Accuser in the original 2014 "Guardians of the&nbsp;
Galaxy," most fans were just getting to know the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:36.160
character — and likely expected a final act fueled&nbsp;
by weapons, explosions and superpowers, not dance.

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:39.440
Singing and shimmying in front of the warlord,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:44.080
Ronan became so confused that it allowed the&nbsp;
other Guardians enough time to blast Ronan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:49.760
and retrieve the Power Stone. It was a memorable&nbsp;
exclamation point on Pratt's now-iconic character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:53.040
and all these years later, the&nbsp;
"Guardians" haven't lost their groove.

00:04:55.280 --> 00:04:59.520
Pop culture has been telling us for more than&nbsp;
three-quarters of a century that superheroes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:03.680
must keep their identities hidden —&nbsp;
the concept even predates Superman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:08.880
stretching back to heroes like Zorro, the&nbsp;
Lone Ranger and the Scarlet Pimpernel. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:13.760
viewers took it for granted in 2008, when the&nbsp;
original "Iron Man" came out, that Robert Downey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:19.440
Jr.'s Tony Stark would do everything in his power&nbsp;
to keep Iron Man's true identity on the down-low.

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:24.480
But the movie ended with Stark addressing the&nbsp;
world's media at a press conference, distancing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:28.960
his gaze from the index cards in his hand and&nbsp;
announcing instead with a twinkle in his eye

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:31.200
"I am Iron Man."

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:36.400
It was not only an early signal that the MCU&nbsp;
was willing to take big swings and diverge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:41.520
from the superhero playbook, but also spoke&nbsp;
volumes about the nature of this cocksure hero.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:46.160
Stark simply isn't the alter-ego type,&nbsp;
and in hindsight, his inability to stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:50.400
himself from taking credit for Iron Man was&nbsp;
perfectly in keeping with who he'd become.

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:56.240
One of the most harrowing moments in the MCU&nbsp;
occurred when Steve Rogers' best friend Bucky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.240 --> 00:06:01.360
Barnes was killed in action during World War&nbsp;
II in "Captain America: The First Avenger."&nbsp;

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:05.120
So it turned out to be a welcomed surprise&nbsp;
when the title villain in "Captain America:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:08.720
The Winter Soldier" was revealed&nbsp;
as Cap's long-lost friend.

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:13.360
Steve's astonishment reflected the feelings&nbsp;
of many viewers, especially since the movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:19.120
was set decades after Steve had last seen Bucky.&nbsp;
The revelations came fast and furious as Bucky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:24.320
was not only alive and looking young, but also&nbsp;
working for HYDRA after being kept cryogenically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.320 --> 00:06:29.600
frozen through the decades. Naturally, Steve and&nbsp;
Bucky's previously established friendship helped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:34.560
goose the rest of the movie, making the pair's&nbsp;
climactic confrontation all the more poignant.

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:40.640
Nick Fury was an integral part of the plot of&nbsp;
"Spider-Man: Far From Home." He was the one who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:45.280
sought Peter Parker out while he was visiting&nbsp;
Europe, recruiting him to team up with Quentin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:50.160
Beck, the up and coming superhero known as&nbsp;
Mysterio, to stop the Elementals. Following&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:55.200
the collapse of S.H.I.E.L.D., Fury was depicted&nbsp;
as having been driven into the shadows, but doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:59.760
his best to continue to enlist superheroes&nbsp;
to eliminate ongoing threats to the world.

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:03.840
So, how shocking was it to learn that&nbsp;
Nick Fury wasn't Nick Fury at all?

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:08.000
The film's post-credits scene brought with it&nbsp;
the revelation that Fury may have been less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:12.240
invested in protecting Earth than the&nbsp;
rest of the movie led fans to believe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:17.200
It showed Fury and his loyal associate Maria&nbsp;
Hill changing into the forms of the Skrulls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:22.080
Talos and Soren. Their conversation then revea&nbsp;
led that Fury had deployed the shape-shifting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:26.880
aliens to do his job while he pursued some kind&nbsp;
of mission in space with the rest of the Skrulls.

00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:29.840
"Everybody back to work!"

00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:36.240
While many of the villains of the MCU haven't made&nbsp;
much of a lasting impression, Michael B. Jordan's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:40.800
take on Eric Killmonger in "Black Panther"&nbsp;
proved to be one of the exceptions. For the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:45.680
first half of the film, the character seemed to&nbsp;
be just an American mercenary after vibranium.

00:07:45.680 --> 00:07:48.960
But ultimately it was revealed that&nbsp;
he had been playing a long game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:54.000
patiently waiting for an excuse to enter Wakanda,&nbsp;
because he was actually T'Challa's cousin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:59.200
and thus had his own claim to the Wakandan throne.&nbsp;
And more: his father had been killed by T'Challa's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.200 --> 00:08:04.320
father, who then abandoned Killmonger and left&nbsp;
him behind in America to fend for himself.

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:07.520
Even though he was willing to destroy&nbsp;
and kill to get what he wanted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:12.560
Killmonger's circumstances and his desire to carry&nbsp;
on his father's mission to help the oppressed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:17.120
made him impossibly sympathetic. In fact,&nbsp;
the fate that awaited him was probably met&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:21.840
by as much disappointment from MCU fans&nbsp;
as any sense of triumphant comeuppance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:26.480
As a result, the character remains one of&nbsp;
the MCU's most potent and memorable villains.

00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:32.720
It's funny how a seemingly throwaway scene in&nbsp;
2015's "Avengers: Age of Ultron" ultimately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.720 --> 00:08:37.200
became the one best remembered from the film.&nbsp;
Perhaps it's because the moment was thrilling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:40.960
quite funny and would later pay off&nbsp;
with a major reward — all emblematic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:44.960
of the unique formula MCU films&nbsp;
tap into at their finest moments.

00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:49.520
During a party at Stark Tower early in the&nbsp;
film, the Avengers jokingly attempted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.520 --> 00:08:53.680
lift Thor's hammer, Mjolnir. As we all&nbsp;
know, the ability to wield the hammer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.680 --> 00:08:58.400
isn't just a simple matter of strength —&nbsp;
the individual has to be worthy to do so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.400 --> 00:09:03.520
with a combination of heroic qualities that&nbsp;
Mjolnir can magically detect. So, it came as no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.520 --> 00:09:08.320
surprise when none of them could lift the hammer,&nbsp;
though it did twitch when Captain America tried.

00:09:08.320 --> 00:09:10.480
But later in the movie, it's revealed there's one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.480 --> 00:09:14.160
character besides Thor who&nbsp;
can handle Mjolnir: Vision.

00:09:14.160 --> 00:09:17.680
After stealing a synthetic body&nbsp;
Ultron had created for himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.680 --> 00:09:21.840
Stark and Banner uploaded Stark's loyal&nbsp;
A.I. program J.A.R.V.I.S. into it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:25.360
with Thor channeling the lightning&nbsp;
necessary to activate the body.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.360 --> 00:09:30.240
Of course, many of the Avengers were suspicious&nbsp;
of this new being they dubbed Vision. After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:35.440
he had at least some of Ultron's consciousness&nbsp;
inside him, so how could he possibly be trusted?

00:09:35.440 --> 00:09:38.320
Easy answer: by effortlessly&nbsp;
lifting Thor's hammer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.320 --> 00:09:43.200
Vision immediately proved himself worthy of&nbsp;
their trust, and had fans cheering in the aisles.

00:09:44.240 --> 00:09:48.560
It seemed inevitable that at least one or two&nbsp;
characters wouldn't make it through "Avengers:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.560 --> 00:09:52.640
Infinity War," yet few could have predicted&nbsp;
the movie opening with the slaughter of at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.640 --> 00:09:57.520
least half of what remained of Asgard — and the&nbsp;
main villain of the MCU up until that point.

00:09:57.520 --> 00:10:01.760
After Thor's home was destroyed during the&nbsp;
events of "Thor: Ragnarok," the citizens who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.760 --> 00:10:06.560
remained behind sailed into space, only&nbsp;
to encounter Thanos and his followers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.560 --> 00:10:10.160
The sequence became even more&nbsp;
shocking when the newly reformed Loki&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.160 --> 00:10:14.080
attempted to kill Thanos, only&nbsp;
to be strangled by the Mad Titan.

00:10:14.080 --> 00:10:16.960
Thankfully, through a mix of&nbsp;
time travel and multiversing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.960 --> 00:10:22.080
an alternate universe Loki would eventually&nbsp;
live on in a self-titled Disney+ series.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.080 --> 00:10:26.560
But that didn't lessen the pain of losing the Loki&nbsp;
who went through "Ragnarok" in "Infinity War."

00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:32.320
In "Captain America: Civil War" Helmut Zemo&nbsp;
went to work employing what he knew about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:36.720
the Avengers to tear them apart. Key to his&nbsp;
plan was showing Tony Stark a video from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:41.760
night in 1991 when his parents died in a car&nbsp;
crash, as it showed that their deaths happened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.760 --> 00:10:46.000
at the hands of Bucky Barnes, acting on&nbsp;
behalf of HYDRA as the Winter Soldier.

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:50.960
While viewers had seen Bucky being sent to carry&nbsp;
out the Starks' executions earlier in the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.960 --> 00:10:54.960
the knowledge he had killed them was trumped&nbsp;
by the heart-pounding slow-burn sequence where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.960 --> 00:11:00.880
Tony learned the truth. Viewers, Captain America,&nbsp;
and Bucky had all hoped Tony wouldn't find out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.880 --> 00:11:05.840
given it wouldn't do him or the other Avengers&nbsp;
any good, and it was gut-wrenching to see Cap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.840 --> 00:11:09.920
and Iron Man turn on each other, their&nbsp;
friendship shattered in a single moment.

00:11:11.280 --> 00:11:14.160
"He's my friend." "So was I."

00:11:15.120 --> 00:11:19.440
When Peter Parker picked up his crush Liz at her&nbsp;
house for the homecoming dance in "Spider-Man:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.440 --> 00:11:22.720
Homecoming," he was as shocked as&nbsp;
the audience when Adrian Toomes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.720 --> 00:11:27.600
the illegal arms dealer he had been trying to&nbsp;
stop, was revealed as Liz's father. Could Peter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.600 --> 00:11:32.800
keep his wits and get out of the situation with&nbsp;
his life, secret identity, and prom date intact?

00:11:32.800 --> 00:11:36.480
The moment is magnificently&nbsp;
orchestrated by director Jon Watts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.480 --> 00:11:40.960
who builds tremendous tension with things as&nbsp;
simple as rear-mirror close-ups and the shades&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.960 --> 00:11:45.920
reflected from street lights. On the surface, it&nbsp;
appears as though Parker still has the advantage;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.920 --> 00:11:51.040
as with any Keaton character, however, this man&nbsp;
is no fool. You can see the wheels turning in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.040 --> 00:11:55.840
his mind as the light of recognition finally&nbsp;
illuminated the deadly confrontation to come.

00:11:56.520 --> 00:12:03.200
"You walk through those doors, you forget any&nbsp;
of this happened, and don't you ever, ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.400 --> 00:12:06.000
interfere with my business again."

00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:12.880
Peter Quill lost his mother to cancer at a young&nbsp;
age and never knew his father... until "Guardians&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.880 --> 00:12:17.600
of the Galaxy 2," when he learned that his&nbsp;
father was the Celestial Ego the Living Planet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.600 --> 00:12:21.680
and that Peter was in possession of the&nbsp;
same extraordinary powers as his dad.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.680 --> 00:12:25.440
Initially, Ego came across as the warm&nbsp;
parent Quill had been missing for most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.440 --> 00:12:28.960
of his life — but eventually,&nbsp;
his true nature became known.

00:12:28.960 --> 00:12:33.440
Ego revealed that he was responsible for&nbsp;
causing Quill's mother's illness and death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.440 --> 00:12:38.480
something neither Quill nor viewers expected.&nbsp;
After all, Ego had claimed he loved Quill's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.480 --> 00:12:43.280
mother. Yet, as Ego himself would point out, when&nbsp;
you're a Celestial who is millions of years old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.280 --> 00:12:48.320
and only getting older, you don't experience&nbsp;
love like a mere mortal. In Ego's mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.320 --> 00:12:52.880
this may have made his actions reasonable —&nbsp;
but for Peter, it was the ultimate betrayal.

00:12:53.680 --> 00:12:58.720
Looking back at the breadth of the MCU, there have&nbsp;
certainly been plenty of fan-service moments. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.720 --> 00:13:02.320
it's hard to think of one that garnered more&nbsp;
applause and excitement than the "Avengers:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.320 --> 00:13:05.840
Endgame" scene when Captain&nbsp;
America proved himself worthy.

00:13:05.840 --> 00:13:10.160
Remember how Cap was able to budge Thor's&nbsp;
hammer way back in "Avengers: Age of Ultron?"&nbsp;

00:13:10.160 --> 00:13:14.320
Well, by the time "Avengers: Endgame"&nbsp;
arrived, the stakes had become so much higher,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.320 --> 00:13:17.520
as the team tried desperately to&nbsp;
restore the half of all life that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.520 --> 00:13:23.200
Thanos had snapped away. So when the trio of&nbsp;
Thor, Cap, and Iron Man confronted Thanos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.200 --> 00:13:27.520
with the Mad Titan moments from ove–rcoming&nbsp;
Thor, Cap's revelation that he may have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.520 --> 00:13:31.760
more worthy than he let on back in "Ultron"&nbsp;
brought joy to both Thor and viewers.

00:13:33.360 --> 00:13:34.160
"I knew it!"

00:13:35.280 --> 00:13:39.760
"The Avengers" was the first time Marvel's&nbsp;
various superheroes teamed up on the big screen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.760 --> 00:13:42.880
Given the excitement of seeing the&nbsp;
team assemble for the first time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.880 --> 00:13:48.320
fans didn't anticipate the emotional gut-punch&nbsp;
of seeing Phase 1 fan favorite Phil Coulson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.320 --> 00:13:50.800
end up on the wrong side of Loki's scepter.

00:13:50.800 --> 00:13:53.200
Coulson's death gave the film real stakes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.200 --> 00:13:57.360
and the uneasy team of superheroes something&nbsp;
to rally around. For fans who loved the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.360 --> 00:14:02.000
steadfast agent, though, Coulson's demise&nbsp;
gave "The Avengers" extra emotional heft.

00:14:02.000 --> 00:14:06.800
Of course, he went on to lead the TV show "Agents&nbsp;
of S.H.I.E.L.D.," making Coulson not only one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.800 --> 00:14:12.640
the first iconic MCU characters to die, but also&nbsp;
the first to be resurrected. And more than once!

00:14:13.840 --> 00:14:18.480
Nick Fury and S.H.I.E.L.D. became major&nbsp;
forces throughout the first many MCU movies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.480 --> 00:14:21.680
Throughout that time, fans had been&nbsp;
led to believe the organization was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.680 --> 00:14:26.480
force for good — as did the Avengers. Such&nbsp;
clear lines of good and evil came crashing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.480 --> 00:14:30.560
down with "Captain America: The Winter&nbsp;
Soldier," however, amidst revelations that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.560 --> 00:14:34.200
for decades a parasite had been feeding&nbsp;
on S.H.I.E.L.D. from the inside: HYDRA.

00:14:34.200 --> 00:14:34.800
"Hail Hyrda!"

00:14:37.760 --> 00:14:41.840
When Captain America and Black Widow went&nbsp;
to seek answers at a S.H.I.E.L.D. facility,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.840 --> 00:14:46.640
they found themselves in an underground lair.&nbsp;
There, a computer with Hydra scientist Arnim&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.640 --> 00:14:51.520
Zola's mind explained Hydra's decades of work&nbsp;
within S.H.I.E.L.D., an admission that allowed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.520 --> 00:14:56.240
him to brag while distracting the heroes from the&nbsp;
missiles on their way to destroy the facility.

00:14:56.240 --> 00:14:59.840
Ultimately, it took the destruction of&nbsp;
S.H.I.E.L.D. to eliminate the threat from Hydra.

00:15:00.560 --> 00:15:04.800
Given all the battles he has fought in the&nbsp;
MCU, it's easy to forget that Peter Parker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.800 --> 00:15:09.360
is still a teenager. So when the revelation&nbsp;
of his identity as the man behind Spider-Man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.360 --> 00:15:13.600
compromised the college acceptances of him&nbsp;
and his friends in "Spider-Man: No Way Home,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.600 --> 00:15:16.480
it made sense that he wouldn't think&nbsp;
through all the possibilities to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.480 --> 00:15:21.280
remedy the situation — and that Doctor Strange&nbsp;
wouldn't double check that Peter had exhausted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.280 --> 00:15:25.200
every other option before coming to him&nbsp;
for a magical solution to his problem.

00:15:25.760 --> 00:15:29.920
Unfortunately, by the time Strange completed&nbsp;
the spell to make the world forget Parker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.920 --> 00:15:34.320
was Spider-Man, things had gone haywire and&nbsp;
the door had been opened to other universes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:34.320 --> 00:15:37.760
It would be a mess that could only&nbsp;
be undone with a very adult decision.

00:15:37.760 --> 00:15:42.560
By the end of the film, Peter had worked with&nbsp;
Strange to finally make everyone forget about him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.560 --> 00:15:47.600
It was a choice that left him isolated, alone,&nbsp;
and clearly entering a new era for the hero.

00:15:48.640 --> 00:15:53.920
The third "Spider-Man" film in the MCU was packed&nbsp;
with surprises, from the web-head teaming up with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.920 --> 00:15:59.040
his counterparts from the previous "Spider-Man"&nbsp;
franchises to a "Venom" series tie-in. But one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.040 --> 00:16:03.040
of the most unexpected moments in "No Way&nbsp;
Home" turned out to be much less about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.040 --> 00:16:08.320
the stunt-casting and more about hard-earned&nbsp;
audience affection for an ill-fated character.

00:16:08.320 --> 00:16:13.680
In every Spider-Man story prior to the MCU, the&nbsp;
death of Uncle Ben had always been a catalyst for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.680 --> 00:16:19.120
Peter to become a hero. But without a Ben in&nbsp;
his origin story, the MCU's Peter had instead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.120 --> 00:16:24.240
been in the care of Aunt May— and had seemed to&nbsp;
discover his fate without a comparable tragedy.

00:16:24.240 --> 00:16:25.200
Until now.

00:16:25.200 --> 00:16:30.640
When May encourages Peter to help the villains who&nbsp;
have arrived in the MCU from alternate universes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.640 --> 00:16:34.880
instead of sending them home to die, Peter&nbsp;
follows her advice. But when Green Goblin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.880 --> 00:16:40.320
takes control of Norman Osborn's body, Aunt&nbsp;
May pays the price. Among her final words?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:40.320 --> 00:16:43.360
"With great power, there must&nbsp;
come great responsibility."

